{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-123307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{3{n}^{4}-{n}^{2}}{5-2{n}^{4}}$ for $n\\ge 1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061665/state.json" "file_sha256" "541713f9f7b1e3b50f7c40d5adec82528678d940cd4be736df2528b5b745ee66"} {"question" "q-123307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\frac{3{n}^{4}-{n}^{2}}{5-2{n}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-2{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{3{n}^{4}-{n}^{2}}{5-2{n}^{4}} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{3{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{{n}^{2}}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{5}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{2{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(3-\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}})}{\\frac{5}{{n}^{4}}-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-0}{0-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit exists, the sequence converges, and its limit is $-\\frac{3}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061665/state.json" "file_sha256" "541713f9f7b1e3b50f7c40d5adec82528678d940cd4be736df2528b5b745ee66"} {"question" "q-123307" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061665/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bc039272919798b69d3d066c73973803da756f0648a0dab88efccb0ab12270f0"} {"question" "q-123307" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061665/state.json" "file_sha256" "541713f9f7b1e3b50f7c40d5adec82528678d940cd4be736df2528b5b745ee66"} {"question" "q-123307" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{3}{2}"] ["math" "0"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{3}{5}"] ["text" "The sequence diverges"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061665/state.json" "file_sha256" "541713f9f7b1e3b50f7c40d5adec82528678d940cd4be736df2528b5b745ee66"} {"question" "q-123307" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{3}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061665/state.json" "file_sha256" "541713f9f7b1e3b50f7c40d5adec82528678d940cd4be736df2528b5b745ee66"} {"question" "q-347970" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{-8n+7}{4n}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061665/state.json" "file_sha256" "541713f9f7b1e3b50f7c40d5adec82528678d940cd4be736df2528b5b745ee66"} {"question" "q-347970" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{-8n+7}{4n}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $4n,$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by $n$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{-8n+7}{4n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{-8n}{n}+\\frac{7}{n})}{\\frac{4n}{n}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{-8+\\frac{7}{n}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8+0}{4} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the sequence converges, and its limit is $-2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061665/state.json" "file_sha256" "541713f9f7b1e3b50f7c40d5adec82528678d940cd4be736df2528b5b745ee66"} {"question" "q-347970" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061665/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bc039272919798b69d3d066c73973803da756f0648a0dab88efccb0ab12270f0"} {"question" "q-347970" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061665/state.json" "file_sha256" "541713f9f7b1e3b50f7c40d5adec82528678d940cd4be736df2528b5b745ee66"} {"question" "q-347970" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061665/state.json" "file_sha256" "541713f9f7b1e3b50f7c40d5adec82528678d940cd4be736df2528b5b745ee66"} {"question" "q-347981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{2-7{n}^{3}}{4{n}^{4}+n}$ for $n\\ge 1?$\n\n${lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061665/state.json" "file_sha256" "541713f9f7b1e3b50f7c40d5adec82528678d940cd4be736df2528b5b745ee66"} {"question" "q-347981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{2-7{n}^{3}}{4{n}^{4}+n}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $4{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{2-7{n}^{3}}{4{n}^{4}+n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{7{n}^{3}}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{4{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}+\\frac{n}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{7}{n}}{4+\\frac{1}{{n}^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-0}{4+0} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit exists, the sequence converges, and its limit is $0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061665/state.json" "file_sha256" "541713f9f7b1e3b50f7c40d5adec82528678d940cd4be736df2528b5b745ee66"} {"question" "q-347981" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061665/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bc039272919798b69d3d066c73973803da756f0648a0dab88efccb0ab12270f0"} {"question" "q-347981" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061665/state.json" "file_sha256" "541713f9f7b1e3b50f7c40d5adec82528678d940cd4be736df2528b5b745ee66"} {"question" "q-347981" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061665/state.json" "file_sha256" "541713f9f7b1e3b50f7c40d5adec82528678d940cd4be736df2528b5b745ee66"} {"question" "q-123309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{2n+1}{7n-5{n}^{2}}$ for $n\\ge 1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061665/state.json" "file_sha256" "541713f9f7b1e3b50f7c40d5adec82528678d940cd4be736df2528b5b745ee66"} {"question" "q-123309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To determine whether the sequence converges or diverges, we need to determine the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\frac{2n+1}{7n-5{n}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe dominant term in the denominator is $-5{n}^{2},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{2n+1}{7n-5{n}^{2}} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2n}{{n}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}})}{(\\frac{7n}{{n}^{2}}-\\frac{5{n}^{2}}{{n}^{2}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{n}+\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}})}{\\frac{7}{n}-5} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+0}{0-5} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limit exists, the sequence converges, and its limit is $0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061665/state.json" "file_sha256" "541713f9f7b1e3b50f7c40d5adec82528678d940cd4be736df2528b5b745ee66"} {"question" "q-123309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14061665/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bc039272919798b69d3d066c73973803da756f0648a0dab88efccb0ab12270f0"} {"question" "q-123309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14